\begin_layout Title
Chapter 22: Duration Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
22.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Some response variables in economics come in the form of a 
\series bold
duration
\series default
, which is the time elapsed until a certain event occurs.
 A few examples include weeks unemployed, months spent on welfare, days
 until arrest after incarceration, and quarters until an Internet firm files
 for bankrupcy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Duration analysis has its origins in what is typically called 
\series bold
survival analysis
\series default
, where the duration of interest is survival time of a subject.
 In survival analysiss we are interested in how various treaments or demographic
 characteristics affect surivval times.
 In the social sciences, we are interested in any situation where an individual,
 beings in an initial state and is either observed to exit the state or
 is censored.
 The calendar dates on which units enter the initial state is either observed
 to exit the state or is censored.
 The calendar dates on which units enter the initial state do not have to
 be the same.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Traditional duration analysis begins by specifying a population distribution
 for the duration, usually conditional on some explanatory variances (covariates
) observed at the beginning of the duration.
 Then we specify a distribution for the duration conditional on the covariates.
 Any reasonable distribution reflects the fact that an unemployment duration
 is nonnegative.
 Once a comple conditional distribution has been specified, the same maximum
 likelihood methods we studied in Chpater 19 for censored regression models
 can be used.
 In this framework, we are typically interested in estimating the effects
 of the covariates on the expected duration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Recent treatments of duration analysis tend to focus on the hazard funciton.
 The hazard function allows us to approximate the proability of exiting
 the initial state within a short interval, conditional on having survived
 up to the starting time of the interval.
 In econometric applications, hazard funcitons are usually conditional on
 some covariates.
 An important feature for policy analysis is allowing the hazard funciton
 to depend on covariates that change over time.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
22.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hazard Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The hazard function plays a central role in modern duration analysis.
 In this section, we discuss various features of the hazard funciton, both
 with and without covariates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
22.2.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hazard Functions Without Covariates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Often in this chapter it is convenient to distinguish random variables from
 particular outcomes of random variables.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T\geq0$
\end_inset

 denote the duration, which has some distribution in the population; 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 denotes a particular value of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 (As with any econometric analysis, it is important to be very clear about
 the relevant population.) In survival analysis, 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is the length of time a subject lives.
 Much of the current terminology in duration analysis comes from survival
 applications.
 For us, 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is the time at which a person leave the initial state.
 For example, if the initial state is unemployment, 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 woudl be the time, measured in, say, weeks, until a person becomes employed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The cumulative distribution function (cdf) of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(t)=P(T\leq t),\ t\geq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The 
\series bold
survivor function
\series default
 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $S(t)\equiv1-F(t)=P(T>t)$
\end_inset

, and this is the probability of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

surviving
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 past time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 We assume in the rest of this section that 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is continuous, and has a differentiable cdf because this assumption simplifies
 statements of certain probabilities.
 Discreteness in observed durations can be viewed as a consequence of the
 sampling scheme.
 Denote the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $f(t)=\frac{dF}{dt}(t)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For 
\begin_inset Formula $h>0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(t\leq T<t+h|T\geq t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

is the probability of leaving the inital state in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[t,t+h)$
\end_inset

 given survival up until time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 The 
\series bold
hazard function
\series default
 for 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t)={\displaystyle \lim_{h\rightarrow0}}\frac{P(t\leq T<t+h|T\geq t)}{h}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(t)$
\end_inset

 is the instantaneous rate of leaving per unit of time.
 From equation (3) it follows that, for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

small
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 h,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(t\leq T\leq t+h|T\geq t)\approx\lambda(t)h
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, the hazard function can be used to approximate a conditional probability
 in much the same way that the hieght of the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 can be used to approximate an unconditional probability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can express the hazard function in terms of the density and cdf very
 simply.
 First, write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(t\leq T<t+h|T\geq t)=\frac{P(t\leq T<t+h)}{P(T\geq t)}=\frac{F(t+h)-F(t)}{1-F(t)}
\]

\end_inset

When the cdf is differentiable, we can take the limit of the right-hand
 side, divided by 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, as 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 approaches zero from above:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t)={\displaystyle \lim_{h\rightarrow0}}\frac{F(t+h)-F(t)}{h}\cdot\frac{1}{1-F(t)}=\frac{f(t)}{1-F(t)}=\frac{f(t)}{S(t)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Because the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $S(t)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $-f(t)$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t)=-\frac{d\log S(t)}{dt}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and, using 
\begin_inset Formula $F(0)=0$
\end_inset

, we can integrate to get
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
F(t)=1-\exp\left[-{\displaystyle \int_{0}}^{t}\lambda(s)\ ds\right],\ t\geq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Straightforward differentiation of equation (7) gives the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(t)=\lambda(t)\exp\left[-{\displaystyle \int_{0}^{t}}\lambda(s)\ ds\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, all proabilities can be computed using the hazard function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The shape of the hazard funciton is of primary interest in many empirical
 applications.
 In the simplest case, the hazard function is a constant
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t)=\lambda,\ \forall\ t\geq0
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This function means that the process driving 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
memoryless
\emph default
: the probability of exit in the next interval does not depend on how much
 time has been spend in the initial state.
 From equation (7), a constant hazard implies
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
F(t)=1-\exp(-\lambda t)
\]

\end_inset

which is the cdf of the 
\series bold
exponential distribution
\series default
.
 Conversely, if 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 has an exponential distribution, it has a constant hazard.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When the hazard funciton is not constant, we say that the process exhibits
 
\series bold
duration dependence
\series default
.
 Assuming that 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is differentialbe, there is 
\series bold
positive duration
\series default
 
\series bold
dependence
\series default
 at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{d\lambda(t)}{dt}>0$
\end_inset

; if 
\begin_inset Formula $\nicefrac{d\lambda(t)}{dt}>0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $t>0$
\end_inset

, then the process exhibits posititive duration dependence.
 With positive duration dependence, the probability of exiting the initial
 state increases the longer one is in the initial state.
 If the derivative is negative, then there is 
\series bold
negative duration dependence
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 has a 
\series bold
Weibull distribution
\series default
, its cdf is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t)=1-\exp(-\gamma t^{\alpha})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 are nonnegative parameters.
 The density is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(t)=\gamma\alpha t^{\alpha-1}\exp(-\gamma t^{\alpha}).$
\end_inset

 By equation (5), the hazard function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t)=\nicefrac{f(t)}{S(t)}=\gamma\alpha t^{\alpha-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

, the Weibull distribution reduces to the exponential with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\gamma$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>1$
\end_inset

, the hazard is monotonically increasing, so the hazard everywhere exhibits
 positive duration dependence; for 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha<1$
\end_inset

, the hazard is monotonically decreasing.
 Provided we think the hazard is monotonically increasing or decreasing,
 the Weibull distribution is a relatively simple way to caputre the duration
 dependence.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We often want to specify the hazard directly, in which case we can use equation
 (7) to determine the duration distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
22.2.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hazard Functions Conditional on Time-Invariant Covariates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Usually in economics we are interested in hazard functions conditional on
 a set of covariates or regressors.
 When these do not change over time, as is often the case given the way
 many duration data sets are collected, then we simply define the hazard
 (and all other features of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

) conditional on the covariates.
 Thus, the conditional hazard is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda(t;x)={\displaystyle \lim_{h\rightarrow0}}\frac{P(t\leq T<t+h|T\geq t,x)}{h}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is a vector of explanatory variables.
 All the formulas from the previous subsection continue to hold provided
 the cdf and density are defined conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 For example, if the conditional cdf 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\cdot|x)$
\end_inset

 is differentialbe, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t;x)=\frac{f(t|x)}{1-F(t|x)}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x)$
\end_inset

 is the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

.
 Often we are interested in the partial effects of the 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda(t;x)$
\end_inset

, which are defined as partial derivatives for continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 and as differences for discrete 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If the durations start at different calendar dates, which is usually the
 case, we can include indicators for different starting dates in the covariates.
 These allow us to control for seasonal differences in duration distributions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An especially important class of models with time-invariant regressors consists
 of 
\series bold
proportional hazard models.

\series default
 A proportional hazard model can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda(t;x)=\kappa(x)\lambda_{0}(t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa(\cdot)>0$
\end_inset

 is a positive funciton of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}(t)>0$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\series bold
baseline hazard
\series default
.
 The baseline hazard is common to all units in the population; individual
 hazard functions differ proportionately based on a function 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa(x)$
\end_inset

 of observed covariates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Typically, 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa(\cdot)$
\end_inset

 is parameterized as 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa(x)=\exp(x^{\prime}\beta)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 is a vector of parameters.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\log\lambda(t;x)=x^{\prime}\beta+\log\lambda_{0}(t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 measures the semielasticity of the hazard with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the log of an underlying variable 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 is the elasticity of the hazard with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Occasionally we are interested only in how the covariates shift the hazard
 funciton, in which case estimation of 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}$
\end_inset

 is not necessary.
 In economics, much of the time we are interested in the shape of the baseline
 hazard.
 If in the Weibull hazard function (10) we replace 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(x^{\prime}\beta)$
\end_inset

, where the first element of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 is unity, we obtain a proportional hazard model with 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{0}(t)\equiv\alpha t^{\alpha-1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
22.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Analysis of Single-Spell Data With Time-Invariant Covariates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume that the population of interest is individuals entering the initial
 state during a given interval of time, say 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,b]$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $b>0$
\end_inset

 is a known constant.
 (Naturally, 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

individual
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 can be replaced with any population unit of interest, such as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

family
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

firm.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) As in all econometric contexts, it is very important to be explicit about
 the underlying population.
 By convention, we let zero denote the earliest canedar ate that an individual
 can enter the initial state, and 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 is the last possible date.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In using the methods of this section, we typically ignor the fact that durations
 are often grouped into discrete intervals and treat them as continously
 distributed.
 If we want to explicitly recognize the discreteness of the measured durations,
 we should treat them as grouped data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We restrict attention to 
\series bold
single-spell data
\series default
.
 That is, we use, at most, one completed spell per individual.
 If, after leaving the initial state, an individual subsequently reenters
 the initial state in the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,b]$
\end_inset

, we ignore this information.
 In addition, covariates in the analysisis are time invariant, which means
 we collect covariates on individuals at a given point in time, usually
 at the beginning of the spell, and we do not re-collect data on the covariates
 during the course of the spell.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
22.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Flow Sampling
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With 
\series bold
flow sampling
\series default
, we sample individuals who enter the state at some point during the interval
 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,b]$
\end_inset

, and we record the length of time each individual is in the initial state.
 We collect data on covariates known at the time the individual entered
 the initial state.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Flow data are usually subject to 
\series bold
right cenoring
\series default
.
 That is, after a certain amount of time, we stop following the individuals
 in the sample, which we must do in order to analyze the data.
 (Right censoring is the only kind that occurs with flow data, so we will
 often refer to right censoring as 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

censoring
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in this and the next subsection.) For individuals who have completed their
 spells in the initial state, we observe the exact duration.
 But for those still in the initial state state, we only know that the durating
 lasted as long as the tracking period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
22.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Maximum Likelihood Estimation With Censored Flow Data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a random draw 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from the population, let 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}\in[0,b]$
\end_inset

 denot the time at which individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 enters the initial state (the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

starting time
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

), let 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 denote the length of time in the initial state (the duration), and let
 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 denote the vector of observed covariates.
 We assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 has a continuous conditional density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(t|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is the vector of unknown parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Without right censoring we would observe a random sample 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{i},t_{i}^{*},x_{i})$
\end_inset

, and estimation would be a standard exercise in conditional maximum liklihood.
 To account for right censoring, we assume that the observed duration, 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}$
\end_inset

, is obtained as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
t_{i}=\min(t_{i}^{*},c_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is the censoring time for individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 In some cases, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is constant across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We assume that, conditional on the covariates, the true duration is independent
 of the starting point, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

, and the censoring time, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
D(t_{i}^{*}|x_{i},a_{i},c_{i})=D(t_{i}^{*}|x_{i})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $D(\cdot|\cdot)$
\end_inset

 denotes the conditional distribution.
 Assumption (15) clearly holds when 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 are constant for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, but it holds under much weaker assumptions.
 Sometimes 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is constant for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, in which case assumption (15) holds when the duration is independent of
 the starting time, conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If there are seasonal effects on the duration, then we may have to put
 dummy variables for different starting dates in 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 to ensure that assumption (15) holds.
 This approach would ensure that assumption (15) holds when a fixed calendar
 date is used for censoring implying that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not constant across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Assumption (15) holds for certian nonstandard censoring schemes, too.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under assumption (15), the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},a_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 does not depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $(a_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, if the duration is not censored, the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}=t_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},a_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

 is simply 
\begin_inset Formula $f(t|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 The probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}$
\end_inset

 is censored is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(t_{i}^{*}\geq c_{i}|x_{i})=1-F(c_{i}|x_{i};\theta)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $F(t|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the conditional cdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Letting 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a censoring indicator (
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if uncensored, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}=0$
\end_inset

 if censored), the conditional likelihood for observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(t_{i}|x_{i};\theta)^{d_{i}}\left[1-F(t_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]^{(1-d_{i})}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Importantly, neither the starting times, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

, nor the length of the interval, 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

, plays a role in the analysis.
 (in fact, in the vast majority of treatments of flow data, 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 are not even introduced.
 However, it is important to know that the reason 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not relevant for the analysis of flow data is the conditional independence
 assumption in equation (15).) By contrast, the censoring times 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 do appear in the likelihood for censored observations because then 
\begin_inset Formula $t_{i}=c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Given data on 
\begin_inset Formula $(t_{i},d_{i},x_{i})$
\end_inset

 for a random sample of size 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

, the maximum likelihood estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is obtained by maximizing
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{N}}\left\{ d_{i}\log\left[f(t_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]+\left(1-d_{i}\right)\log\left[1-F(t_{i}|x_{i};\theta)\right]\right\} 
\end{equation}

\end_inset

For the choices of 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x;\theta)$
\end_inset

 used in practice, the conditional MLE regularity conditions old, and the
 MLE is 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{N}$
\end_inset

-consistent and asymptotically normal.
 (If there is no censoring, 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and the second term in expression (17) is dropped.) Because the hazard function
 can be expressed as in equation (11), once we specify 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

, the hazard function can be estimated once we have the MLE, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\theta}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

